===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|impersonal|childish|or|nonstandard|intransitive}} To produce lightning.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1916|author=Dorothy Canfield Fisher|title=Understood Betsy|passage=Or if it thundered and '''lightninged''', Aunt Frances always dropped everything she might be doing and held Elizabeth Ann tightly in her arms until it was all over.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1968|author=Dan Greenburg|title=Chewsday: a sex novel|passage=The next day, though it is not only raining but thundering and '''lightninging''' as well, antiquing is seen by three-fourths of those present as a lesser evil than free play.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1987|author=Tricia Springstubb|title=Eunice Gottlieb and the unwhitewashed truth about life|passage="Hey!" yelled Reggie, pulling her back. "Get in here! It's '''lightninging'''. I don't want a charcoal-broiled friend!"}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1988|author=Carlo Collodi; Roberto Innocenti|title=The adventures of Pinocchio
|passage=I don't know, Father, but believe me, it has been a horrible night — one that I'll never forget. It thundered and '''lightninged''', and I was very hungry.}}

====Usage notes====
* The standard, but rare, verb for "produce lightning" is {{m|en|lighten}}, used only in the impersonal form "it lightens", or as "it’s lightening".

